WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.963
Why would a healthy ear send sound back out?

2
00:00:06.583 --> 00:00:08.423
And what can a screening test learn from it?

3
00:00:09.317 --> 00:00:15.217
In 1978, Kemp played brief clicks into human ear canals and averaged the response.

4
00:00:16.317 --> 00:00:19.357
A slowly decaying sound remained after each click.

5
00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:23.157
In the ears tested with cochlear deafness, it was absent.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.500
Some emissions come from reflections of the travelling wave.

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.540
Others come from the cochlea's nonlinearity.

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.040
Picture a curve that acts on the sound wave instant by instant.

9
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.500
It passes small inputs and flattens large ones.

10
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.660
Feed it two tones, at 2,000 and 2,400 hertz.

11
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:47.793
Predict. What comes out, besides the two tones you put in?

12
00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:58.783
New tones. Where the two tones add up to a big peak, the curve squashes it.

13
00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:03.377
A cubic term mixes two of the first tone with one of the second.

14
00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:06.977
That makes 1,600 hertz, among other new tones.

15
00:01:07.897 --> 00:01:10.577
In ear-canal tests, that one is usually the largest.

16
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:19.030
Predict again. Play the same tones at a moderate level into a cochlea after death.

17
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:20.830
What comes back?

18
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.970
Usually little or nothing, at these levels.

19
00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.990
The living drive and amplification are gone.

20
00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:37.110
That does not mean every nonlinearity in the transduction channel has vanished.

21
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:39.933
That is the one idea at work.

22
00:01:40.713 --> 00:01:44.173
The same vulnerable process gives the gain and makes the emission.

23
00:01:45.283 --> 00:01:50.483
A fast, saturating curve makes new tones whenever the input reaches its curved part.

24
00:01:51.363 --> 00:01:55.843
What ties this one to outer hair cells is that, at screening levels, it fails when

25
00:01:55.843 --> 00:01:56.843
they fail. A fast, saturating curve makes new tones when they fail.

26
00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.217
Newborn screening uses emissions.

27
00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:03.897
A pass shows outer hair cells can make an emission through the middle ear.

28
00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:06.627
It does not show intact hearing.

29
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:12.047
In auditory neuropathy, the brainstem response can be absent or very abnormal.

30
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.827
One question stays open.

31
00:02:15.707 --> 00:02:17.807
How far along the cochlea is the gain gathered?

32
00:02:18.647 --> 00:02:21.527
A second tone can lower the response to first one.

33
00:02:22.327 --> 00:02:25.987
Changing its frequency changes where it acts, which helps map the gain.

34
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.290
Take this with you.

35
00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.710
A living cochlea's nonlinearity makes new tones, and a microphone in the ear canal

36
00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:34.510
can hear them.
